mmand flags only, not the full prompt with natural-language directives. Users running --print-command may be confused by the multi-line directive appearing in what looks like a command-line invocation.\",\n      \"recommendation\": \"Update the dry-run output to clearly distinguish between the slash command (flags) and the appended directive, or add a comment explaining that the printed prompt includes execution directives. Alternatively, provide a --print-command-only flag that omits directives for scripting use cases.\",\n      \"cross_references\": [\n        \"src/superclaude/cli/reflect/runner.py:461\",\n        \"src/superclaude/cli/reflect/runner.py:465\"\n      ],\n      \"confidence\": \"medium\"\n    },\n    {\n      \"title\": \"Directive text is not parameterized or localized\",\n      \"category\": \"naming\",\n      \"severity_hint\": \"low\",\n      \"file\": \"src/superclaude/cli/reflect/runner.py\",\n      \"line\": 16,\n      \"line_range_end\": 23,\n      \"in_diff\": true,\n      \"evidence\": \"inline_directive = (\\n            \\\"\\\\n\\\\nExecute this reflect run INLINE, yourself, as the top-level orchestrator. \\\"...\",\n      \"why\": \"The directive is a multi-line string literal hardcoded inline. Best practice for this type of instruction is to define it as a module-level constant (e.g., _INLINE_EXECUTION_DIRECTIVE) so it can be reused, tested independently, and easily modified. The inline placement makes it harder to unit-test the directive text (e.g., verify it contains no f-string injection risks, matches expected wording) and harder to locate when debugging.\",\n      \"recommendation\": \"Extract the directive to a module-level constant: _INLINE_EXECUTION_DIRECTIVE = \\\"\\\\n\\\\nExecute this reflect run INLINE...\\\" and reference it in _build_prompt. This improves testability and makes the directive discoverable via grep.\",\n      \"cross_references\": [\n        \"src/superclaude/cli/reflect/runner.py:53\"\n      ],\n      \"c